{#
  THE INVITATION.

  ── THE DIRECTION, IN ONE SENTENCE ──────────────────────────────────────────
  An engraved ceremony invitation: deep ink ground, a single gold rule, a serif
  display face over monospace metadata, letterpress restraint. No gradients, no
  glass, no pill badges, no icon rows.

  ── WHY IT IS NOT THE CAMPAIGN SKELETON ─────────────────────────────────────
  The first version of this rendered through emails/campaign.twig's block list, which
  was the wrong call and looked it: that skeleton is built for a broadcast — a
  countdown hero, numbered asks, a green CTA — and an invitation to be honoured in
  public is not a broadcast. It is addressed to one person, it has one thing to say,
  and the design has to carry that or the words are wasted.

  What it DOES inherit from that skeleton is the scaffolding, deliberately: the
  fluid-hybrid 560px wrapper, the MSO conditional tables, the VML button, the styled
  alt text, presentation roles on every layout table, the hidden preheader, no data:
  URIs. Those twelve properties are held by EmailInboxCompatTest and they are the
  difference between a design and a design that arrives.

  ── THE HIERARCHY, AND WHAT LEFT IT ─────────────────────────────────────────
  Two actions survive on the surface: open your pass, and get your guests' tickets.
  Everything else an invitee might want — the schedule, the venue map, the programme
  — is one tap away on the event page and is NOT here. An invitation with six links
  is a newsletter.

  Type: Georgia for the display voice (present on every mail client that matters and
  with real character — a web font cannot be relied on here); Helvetica/Arial for
  body; Consolas/Courier for the micro-labels and the reference, because a reference
  is read aloud at a door and monospace is what makes it transcribable.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:v="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:vml" xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<meta http-equiv="X-UA-Compatible" content="IE=edge">
{# iOS turns dates, addresses and references into blue links and then restyles them.
   A ceremony date rendered as a system link in the middle of an engraved invitation
   is the single ugliest thing this template can do, so it is switched off. #}
<meta name="format-detection" content="telephone=no, date=no, address=no, email=no, url=no">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="light">
<meta name="supported-color-schemes" content="light">
<title>{{ subject }}</title>
<!--[if mso]>
<xml><o:OfficeDocumentSettings><o:PixelsPerInch>96</o:PixelsPerInch></o:OfficeDocumentSettings></xml>
<style>
  /* Word engine: no rem, and cells need mso-line-height-rule or the leading is ignored.
     Deliberately no angle-bracketed tag names in this comment — the balance check counts
     tags inside an [if mso] block, and a tag named in prose here reads as an unclosed one. */
  table, td, div, p, a { font-family: Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif !important; mso-line-height-rule: exactly; }
</style>
<![endif]-->
<style>
  /* Gmail strips this block in several configurations — POP/IMAP accounts most
     commonly — so nothing below is load-bearing. It refines; it does not lay out. */
  .ag-w { width:100% !important; max-width:560px !important; }
  .ag-px { padding-left:32px !important; padding-right:32px !important; }
  a { color:#8a6205; }
  /* iOS still detects dates and addresses with format-detection set, wraps them in its
     own anchor and restyles them blue. A ceremony date rendered as a system link in the
     middle of an engraved invitation is the ugliest thing this template could do. */
  a[x-apple-data-detectors] {
    color:inherit !important; text-decoration:none !important; font-size:inherit !important;
    font-family:inherit !important; font-weight:inherit !important; line-height:inherit !important;
  }
  @media only screen and (max-width:600px) {
    .ag-px  { padding-left:22px !important; padding-right:22px !important; }
    .ag-d1  { font-size:27px !important; line-height:33px !important; }
    .ag-d2  { font-size:22px !important; line-height:28px !important; }
    .ag-name{ font-size:20px !important; line-height:27px !important; }
    /* Full width, because a 300px button beside a 22px gutter on a 320px screen is a
       button with 2px of margin. The only control in the email, and a thumb has to
       find it without aiming. */
    .ag-btn { width:100% !important; }
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    /* Apple Mail and iOS honour this and leave the rest alone; Gmail and Outlook.com
       invert regardless and do it badly on near-white, which is why the card is a
       warm off-white rather than #ffffff — an inverted #ffffff goes flat black and
       takes the gold with it. */
    .ag-body { background-color:#0b1f22 !important; }
    .ag-card { background-color:#12292c !important; }
    .ag-ink  { color:#f2f4f3 !important; }
    .ag-mute { color:#a8bbba !important; }
    .ag-rule { border-color:#2a4a4c !important; }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body class="ag-body" style="margin:0; padding:0; background-color:#e9ecec; -webkit-text-size-adjust:100%; -ms-text-size-adjust:100%;">

{# The preheader: the line an inbox shows beside the subject. Hidden, and padded with
   zero-width joiners so the client does not pull body copy in after it. #}
<div style="display:none; max-height:0; max-width:0; font-size:1px; line-height:1px; opacity:0; overflow:hidden; mso-hide:all;">
  {{ preheader }}&#8202;&zwnj;&#8202;&zwnj;&#8202;&zwnj;&#8202;&zwnj;&#8202;&zwnj;&#8202;&zwnj;&#8202;&zwnj;
</div>

<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" bgcolor="#e9ecec" class="ag-body" style="background-color:#e9ecec;">
  <tr>
    <td align="center" style="padding:28px 12px 40px 12px;">

      <!--[if mso]>
      <table role="presentation" width="560" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="width:560px;"><tr><td>
      <![endif]-->
      <table role="presentation" class="agw-wrapper ag-w ag-card" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="width:100%; max-width:560px; background-color:#fdfdfc; border:1px solid #d7dcdb;">

        {# ── 1 · THE CEREMONY CARD ─────────────────────────────────────────
           Masthead and hero are ONE ink block, and that is the structural change.
           Before, the ink was a slab in the MIDDLE of the email holding the button,
           which made the heaviest thing on the page something you met after three
           screens of paper. Weight belongs at the top, where it reads as a header
           instead of an interruption. #}
        {# ── ONE HEADER, NOT TWO STACKED ──────────────────────────────────
           The mark used to sit on a WHITE band above this ink block, because the
           lockup is green-on-white artwork and there was no reversed version of
           it. That put three value zones in the first screen — white, ink, paper
           — and a fourth colour, the brand green #006634, which appears nowhere
           else in this email. It read as two designs bolted together, and the
           band was a 560×158 area with one small object alone in it: whitespace
           left over rather than distributed.

           The lockup is exactly two colours, #FFFFFF and #006634, so every pixel
           is a blend of the pair. `logo-africa-gates-gold.png` is that blend
           recoloured — gold where the ink was, transparent where the paper was —
           which is a two-colour treatment rather than an inversion, and an
           inversion is what would give a gold block with a hole in it.

           So the header is one ink zone in the platform's own two colours, and
           the mark belongs to it. #}
        <tr>
          <td bgcolor="#10292c" style="background-color:#10292c; padding:26px 32px 4px 32px;" class="ag-px">
            {# 88 × 101 is the artwork's own 640:734 ratio. A width and height that
               are not the real ratio make a blocked image reserve the wrong box and
               the layout jump when it loads. #}
            <img src="{{ logo_url }}" width="88" height="101" alt="Africa GATES"
                 style="display:block; width:88px; max-width:88px; height:auto; border:0; outline:none; text-decoration:none; font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:15px; line-height:22px; font-weight:bold; color:#f3b416;">
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td bgcolor="#10292c" style="background-color:#10292c; padding:26px 32px 30px 32px;" class="ag-px">
            <p style="margin:0 0 12px 0; font-family:Consolas,'Courier New',monospace; font-size:10px; letter-spacing:2.2px; color:#9fb3b2; text-transform:uppercase;">You are invited &middot; {{ audience_one }}</p>

            {# The EVENT is the specific thing; "You are invited" is the generic thing.
               The generic one used to be the 35px headline and the event a subordinate
               line under it — so the largest type in the email was the part that is the
               same in every invitation ever sent. Swapped. #}
            <h1 class="ag-d1" style="margin:0; font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:33px; line-height:39px; font-weight:normal; color:#ffffff; letter-spacing:-0.5px;">{{ event_title }}</h1>

            {# ── THE DATE, SET AS AN OBJECT ────────────────────────────────
               This is the redesign's centre. An invitation exists to say WHEN, and the
               date was 15px body copy under a 10px label in a two-column table below
               the fold — the least prominent thing on the page. It is now the second
               voice after the event's own name, and it is what somebody screenshots.

               Three parts rather than one sentence, so it can be set typographically:
               a tracked weekday, the date large, the time and place quiet beneath. #}
            {# A SHORT rule, not a full-width one — `width` on a lone td inside a 100%
               table constrains nothing, and a rule spanning the card competes with the
               3px gold band that closes the header eleven lines below. The width goes on
               the table. #}
            <table role="presentation" width="42" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="width:42px; margin-top:22px;">
              <tr><td bgcolor="#f3b416" style="background-color:#f3b416; height:2px; line-height:2px; font-size:0;">&nbsp;</td></tr>
            </table>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0">
              <tr>
                <td style="padding-top:16px;">
                  <p style="margin:0; font-family:Consolas,'Courier New',monospace; font-size:10.5px; letter-spacing:2.6px; color:#f3b416; text-transform:uppercase;">{{ when_day }}</p>
                  <p class="ag-d2" style="margin:7px 0 0 0; font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:25px; line-height:31px; color:#ffffff; letter-spacing:-0.2px;">{{ when_date }}</p>
                  <p style="margin:9px 0 0 0; font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; font-size:14px; line-height:22px; color:#9fb3b2;">{{ when_time }}{% if where %} &middot; {{ where }}{% endif %}</p>
                </td>
              </tr>
            </table>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td bgcolor="#f3b416" style="background-color:#f3b416; height:3px; line-height:3px; font-size:0;">&nbsp;</td></tr>

        {% if cover_url %}
        {# ── 2 · THE EVENING, PICTURED ─────────────────────────────────────
           width AND height on the tag, always: a blocked remote image with no height
           collapses the panel it is the hero of, and Outlook desktop blocks by
           default. The alt text is styled for the same reason — unstyled alt renders
           as 10px serif and reads as a broken attachment rather than a sentence.

           Directly under the rule, so a blocked image leaves the header intact rather
           than a hole in the middle of the letter. #}
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:0; font-size:0; line-height:0;">
            <img src="{{ cover_url }}" width="560" height="220" alt="{{ event_title }}"
                 style="display:block; width:100%; max-width:560px; height:auto; border:0; outline:none; text-decoration:none; font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:14px; line-height:22px; color:#4a5c5e; background-color:#e9ecec;">
          </td>
        </tr>
        {% endif %}

        {# ── 3 · THE ADDRESS AND THE REASON ────────────────────────────────
           Their name used to sit centred, italic, between two full-width hairlines —
           which is the visual language of a CERTIFICATE, and a certificate is a thing
           you are given rather than a thing you are asked. This is a letter to one
           person, so it opens the way a letter does.

           And the reason is sans now, not 17px Georgia at a 29px leading. Long serif
           paragraphs read as a document; the serif is spent on the two moments that
           earn it — the event's name and theirs. #}
        <tr>
          <td class="ag-px" style="padding:34px 32px 0 32px;">
            <p class="ag-name ag-ink" style="margin:0; font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:22px; line-height:29px; color:#10292c;">{{ salutation }} {{ name }},</p>
            <p class="ag-ink" style="margin:16px 0 0 0; font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; font-size:16px; line-height:27px; color:#1e3b3e;">{{ witness }}</p>
            <p class="ag-mute" style="margin:14px 0 0 0; font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; font-size:16px; line-height:27px; color:#4a5c5e;">
              We would like the people who know that work best to be in the room to see it recognised.
            </p>
          </td>
        </tr>

        {# ── 4 · THE PASS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
           The one primary action, on the paper ground. It was inside a dark slab
           competing with the masthead for weight; the ink is the header now, so this
           can simply be the button it always was. Full width on a phone, which is the
           only thing a thumb reliably hits.

           Gold VML for Outlook, real anchor for everything else. #}
        <tr>
          <td class="ag-px" style="padding:30px 32px 0 32px;">
            <!--[if mso]>
            <v:roundrect xmlns:v="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:vml" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" href="{{ id_url }}" style="height:50px; v-text-anchor:middle; width:300px;" arcsize="8%" strokecolor="#f3b416" fillcolor="#f3b416">
              <w:anchorlock/>
              <center style="color:#1a1204; font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; font-size:15.5px; font-weight:bold;">Open your pass</center>
            </v:roundrect>
            <![endif]-->
            <!--[if !mso]><!-->
            <a class="ag-btn" href="{{ id_url }}" style="display:inline-block; background-color:#f3b416; color:#1a1204; font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; font-size:15.5px; font-weight:bold; line-height:50px; text-align:center; text-decoration:none; width:300px; border-radius:4px;">Open your pass</a>
            <!--<![endif]-->
            <p class="ag-mute" style="margin:12px 0 0 0; font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; font-size:13.5px; line-height:21px; color:#5a6d6f;">
              Your seat is arranged &mdash; there is no ticket for you to buy. The pass is a live
              page: open it on your phone at the door.
            </p>
          </td>
        </tr>

        {# ── 5 · THE STUB ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
           Their reference is one string doing two jobs: their identity at the door and
           the code their guests spend. It used to be centred inside a gold-bordered
           box, which reads as a coupon and matches nothing else on the page.

           Drawn as a STUB instead — a dashed edge across the top, the way the pass and
           the printed ticket are perforated. Same object, three surfaces. Left-aligned,
           because everything else on this card is. #}
        <tr>
          <td class="ag-px" style="padding:34px 32px 0 32px;">
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" bgcolor="#f6f7f6" style="background-color:#f6f7f6; border-top:2px dashed #cfd6d5;">
              <tr>
                <td style="padding:18px 20px 18px 20px;">
                  <p style="margin:0 0 9px 0; font-family:Consolas,'Courier New',monospace; font-size:10px; letter-spacing:2.2px; color:#8a6205; text-transform:uppercase;">Bring your people</p>
                  <p class="ag-ink" style="margin:0; font-family:Consolas,'Courier New',monospace; font-size:20px; line-height:26px; letter-spacing:2.2px; color:#10292c;">{{ reference }}</p>
                  <p class="ag-mute" style="margin:8px 0 0 0; font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; font-size:13px; line-height:20px; color:#5a6d6f;">
                    {{ discount }}% off &middot; up to {{ quota }} guests
                  </p>
                </td>
              </tr>
            </table>
            <p class="ag-mute" style="margin:14px 0 0 0; font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; font-size:14.5px; line-height:24px; color:#4a5c5e;">
              Share it with the people you want in the room. It takes {{ discount }}% off their
              tickets, for up to {{ quota }} of them{% if tier_line %}{{ tier_line }}{% endif %}.
              <a href="{{ events_url }}" style="color:#8a6205; text-decoration:underline;">Send them to the tickets &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </td>
        </tr>

        {# ── 9 · SIGN-OFF ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
        <tr>
          <td class="ag-px" style="padding:32px 32px 0 32px;">
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0">
              <tr><td class="ag-rule" style="border-top:1px solid #e2e6e5; font-size:0; line-height:0;">&nbsp;</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p class="ag-ink" style="margin:20px 0 0 0; font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; font-size:15px; line-height:25px; color:#1e3b3e;">
              We would be honoured to have you with us. The formal invitation is attached, for your records.
            </p>
            <p class="ag-ink" style="margin:16px 0 0 0; font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif; font-size:16px; line-height:24px; color:#10292c;">
              With respect,<br>Africa&nbsp;GATES
            </p>
          </td>
        </tr>

        <tr><td style="padding:0 0 38px 0; font-size:0; line-height:0;">&nbsp;</td></tr>
      </table>

      {# ── FOOTER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Outside the card, in monospace, small and quiet. An invitation that ends in
         a marketing footer stops being an invitation. #}
      <table role="presentation" class="ag-w" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="width:100%; max-width:560px;">
        <tr>
          <td align="center" style="padding:20px 24px 0 24px;">
            <p style="margin:0; font-family:Consolas,'Courier New',monospace; font-size:11px; line-height:19px; color:#7a8b8c;">
              {{ postal_address }}<br>
              <a href="{{ unsubscribe_url }}" style="color:#7a8b8c; text-decoration:underline;">Stop receiving email from Africa GATES</a>
            </p>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </table>
      <!--[if mso]></td></tr></table><![endif]-->

    </td>
  </tr>
</table>
</body>
</html>
